  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "note_17",
      "text": "WORKFLOW: re-entrainment after a time-zone shift. Compare this card with an abrupt schedule change: the contrast IS the lesson. The zeitgeber here moves 8 hours in a single step, but the rhythm does not follow in a single step. It runs at a transient period of about 25.3 h for a week, gaining roughly 1.3 h a day, which draws the slow diagonal crawl you can see in the middle of the actogram before the band locks on at the new phase. Those transients are diagnostic: a record that simply jumps to the new schedule is being DRIVEN by it (see the masking card), whereas a record that crawls is a real oscillator being re-entrained. The crawl is also, roughly, why jet lag lasts about a day per time zone.",
      "x": 40,
      "y": 24,
      "width": 360,
      "height": 190
    }
  ],
